P3. Product lattice in the type system
======================================

Compiled on the pinned nightly with:
  no generic_const_exprs, no dyn, no TypeId, no std::any, no_std core.
The only gate is const_trait_impl, which is on the allowed list.

A. 81 of 81 ordered pairs: the type-level join equals the const-fn
   join on tags. Each is a `const _: () = assert!(..)`, so this line
   printing at all means every one of them passed at compile time.

B. exhaustive laws on the const-fn join, computed now:
   idempotence  : 0 failures of 9
   commutativity: 0 failures of 81
   associativity: 0 failures of 729

A and B compose: the type-level join is the const-fn join, and the
const-fn join is a semilattice, so the type-level join is one, without
expanding a macro over every triple.

What this does NOT establish: that these are the right axes, that
three points per axis is the right granularity, or that the two roles
named here are the only ones. It establishes that the SHAPE compiles
and is lawful, which is the question a canon has to answer before it
can write the intent down.
